from write_records import add,N
add('grfr',1,'Broad change remains the goal','Find a more workable framing without turning broad mind change into a smaller action checklist.','A change in representation looks promising for making the program manageable; the endpoint still has to remain broad beneficial change.', '''Original goal captured from the assignment: “autonomous beneficial efficient broad mind change.” This record proposes alternative frames; a frame that changes success is not adopted. The current instruction explicitly preserves the original goal, so no new permission is required to continue scope-preserving work.

Obstacle: the goal includes many kinds of change and an efficiency demand. It is hard to choose and compare operations, not logically contradictory. “Change” can refer to belief, attention, interest, appraisal, role, representation, or action. A single action count would make the endpoint easier to measure by deleting much of it.

Underlying values: beneficial change, breadth, autonomy, and efficient continuing investigation are explicit task values. The personal intrinsic termini are unknown; the separate ve-1 execution preserves that uncertainty and its original source was loaded. No hidden value needs to replace the explicit ones.

Eight reframe candidates with honest preservation assessment:

| Candidate | Type | Achieves | Does not achieve |
|---|---|---|---|
| A portfolio of diverse local transformations with later uptake | Scope | Works on all named change types | Does not prove all possible change has occurred |
| A sequence of small actions | Intent substitution | Observable behavior | Does not preserve non-action changes |
| A list of practical tips | Intent substitution | Convenient suggestions | Does not execute originals or show transfer |
| A map of current beliefs | Scope as one component | Belief-state visibility | Alone does not fulfill broad goal |
| A motivation routine | Value-discovery substitution | Candidate sustained practice | Deletes other targets and unelicited value assumption |
| A time-limited comparison per operation | Scope, conditional | Manages effort | Cannot truncate original required floors |
| An illustrated metaphor of change | Metaphorical component | A new representation | Alone cannot demonstrate requested consequence |
| A project claiming final mental optimization | Complete pivot | A claim of finality | No evidence of exhaustive optimality |

Explicit choice state: original goal remains chosen by the supplied instruction; A and F are admissible implementation frames within it. B, C, E, and H are not adopted. D and G can be components, never substitute endpoints. No absent user acceptance is fabricated.

Later application: the next fun/exploration record is allowed to change a representation or attention target; it is not required to end with a behavioral instruction. A tangible action count would reject such an output even when it changes subsequent analysis. The scope frame keeps it eligible without awarding success in advance.

Certificate: A preserves kinds of success while decomposing its investigation; B removes non-action kinds. Achieving B alone would not achieve the original, so it cannot be called clarification. Strongest contrary branch: action might be a route to broad change; that makes it an instrument, not an equivalent endpoint.''','The portfolio frame is retained as an implementation of the original goal. The central no-substitution judgment was already present, so no new benefit is credited.','Concrete alternative frames are reviewable; easier substituted endpoints remain visible and unchosen.','UNRESOLVED','Content makes eight different success conditions explicit. Organization keeps implementation frames beside the unchanged goal.','Try a representation that changes attention without action; test whether a scope-preserving deadline truncates needed work; compare role and motivation changes as independent targets.',N+' Eight reframe candidates, explicit original-choice state, and a subsequent eligibility judgment.')
add('grfr',2,'An invitation cannot guarantee another person’s response','Test an impossible-looking relationship outcome without replacing it with merely doing one’s part.','Careful invitations can be a reasonable route toward cooperation; a guarantee that every autonomous recipient will cooperate is a much stronger claim.', '''Constructed original goal, verbatim: “Make every collaborator genuinely interested in this project while leaving each fully free to decline interest.” This is not a personal goal attributed to the user.

Obstacle: “every” and “fully free” do not themselves create a logical contradiction—everyone could freely become interested. They do prevent an actor from guaranteeing the outcome across all allowed collaborator states. A stipulated collaborator who values an unrelated project and remains uninterested after a respectful invitation is an allowed state and defeats the universal guarantee.

/ve dependency executed here: what matters about shared interest? Possible chains are shared interest → richer cooperation → collective creation; shared interest → less loneliness → connection; shared interest → less management effort → convenience; shared interest → agreement with proposer → validation. Their intrinsic termini and additional-goal exhaustion are unknown. None authorizes replacing voluntary interest with compliance.

Reframes: (1) offer varied entry points while preserving the original interest aim—scope; (2) find collaborators who already share interest—scope if “every collaborator” permits choosing membership, substitution if it refers to fixed named people; (3) guarantee respectful invitations—intent substitution; (4) require attendance—complete pivot to compliance; (5) build a compelling prototype—scope as instrument, no guarantee; (6) ask people which aspect they find interesting—scope as information gathering; (7) cultivate one’s own interest regardless of others—value substitution; (8) use “everyone lit up” as a metaphor—metaphorical substitution if literal universality was intended.

Honest assessment: only 1, 5, and 6 preserve a fixed group’s voluntary interest goal without redefining success. None removes the countercase. 2 depends on membership interpretation, retained separately rather than silently choosing the easy reading. 3 and 7 can succeed while the original fails. 4 changes both aim and means.

Choice: no real person is asked; for this application the original remains the evaluand and alternatives remain proposals. The exact universal guarantee is rejected. The more modest scope-preserving efforts remain possible but untested.

Later application: generated project invitation offers three substantive entry points—visual design, question testing, and collaborative interpretation—plus an explicit option to decline. Its evaluation records “three invitations available; zero observed interest responses.” It does not rename availability as interest.

Certificate: an allowed uninterested collaborator suffices to refute guaranteed universal interest. The strongest contrary case is a group that all becomes interested; it shows possibility, not control over all allowed states. The goal-as-hope is coherent; the goal-as-guarantee is not established.''','I reject guaranteed universal interest while retaining voluntary interest as a coherent aim. A respectful invitation is not counted as achieving that aim.','The generated invitation preserves reciprocal agency and avoids a success claim unsupported by any response.','REJECT','Content separates possible outcome, controllable contribution, and guarantee. The rejected guarantee remains visible beside viable scope-preserving instruments.','Try a fixed group and an open membership group separately; observe real opt-in responses when available; test a goal that requires mutual disagreement rather than shared enthusiasm.',N+' Eight reframe types, four value chains, two interpretations of membership, and a generated invitation.')
add('grfr',3,'Effortless interest is not the same as low-friction entry','Test whether removing friction preserves a curiosity goal that asks for effortless sustained interest.','Reducing setup cost is a plausible aid to starting exploration, but it does not establish continuous interest.', '''Constructed original goal: “Stay interested in every research question for the whole session without effort.” Original actor is a fictional researcher; no claim about the user’s energy or health is used.

Why difficult: the set includes questions the actor already knows and questions irrelevant to their concerns. The condition “every” forbids selecting only interesting material; “whole session” forbids any period of indifference; “without effort” forbids deliberate persistence being counted as interest. No empirical claim is needed to show a stipulated indifferent interval fails that exact criterion.

Underlying-value /ve execution: candidate chains include effortless work → easier continuation → sustained inquiry; constant interest → enjoyable session → enjoyment; every question retained → no missed discovery → thoroughness; no effort → lower cost → efficiency. All personal termini remain unelicited. Enjoyment and thoroughness can diverge when a required question is dull.

Eight proposals: prepared tools (scope instrument, no guaranteed interest); alternating question types (scope instrument, unknown response); focus only on currently interesting questions (scope reduction); curiosity breaks (changes whole-session condition); tolerate effort for a valued goal (changes no-effort condition); treat “effortless” as metaphor for low friction (only if intended metaphorically); shorten session to one minute (changes duration if fixed); let interest shift while preserving the question record (changes constant-interest condition).

Assessment: prepared tools and varied material reduce external obstacles in the constructed design, not internal interest by definition. The other six achieve different criteria. The literal original stays unachieved; no substitute is marked approved. A metaphorical reading is preserved as a separate candidate, not silently selected.

Later application: the fictional schedule includes a prepared open notebook, two question families, and an optional stopping point. Its outcome fields are “setup readiness = yes,” “actual interest over time = not observed,” and “effortless continuous interest = not established.” The structured output exposes exactly which premise remains missing.

Certificate: the original universal requires all questions and all session intervals to meet the interest/no-effort conjunction. A prepared notebook establishes neither conjunct. The countercase of an already-solved question keeps the literal guarantee unsupported. The useful preparation design is an instrument with its own narrow success metric.''','The literal goal remains unresolved; a prepared environment is not treated as evidence of effortless sustained interest.','The design is concrete while its mental-state outcome remains unclaimed.','UNRESOLVED','Content separates friction, effort, and interest. Organization retains the literal and metaphorical readings as different candidates.','Compare a prepared and unprepared real session if data arrive; explore interest that grows after effort; test whether question selection is an authorized goal component.',N+' Eight reframe candidates and four value chains, with a later three-field outcome record.')
add('funr',1,'A museum of questions nobody has answered','Let a playful representation generate a new kind of question selection rather than demand an answer from every question.','I initially represented a question primarily as a request for an answer; a useful archive therefore seemed to consist of answers and unresolved answer slots.', '''Subject: a museum whose exhibits are questions, including questions that have never had an answer. Curiosity hooks: a museum preserves objects but questions point away from themselves; an unanswered question can outlive its asker; a label can spoil an exhibit by explaining it; an empty pedestal can attract attention; two visitors can leave with incompatible good questions.

Thread: what does a question exhibit contain?

Pull 1: an answer cannot be the defining exhibit because the museum includes unanswered questions. Finding: the exhibit must preserve the conditions that make the question noticeable.
Pull 2: what survives if words are removed? Finding: a door handle mounted on a blank wall asks about absent access without a sentence.
Pull 3: what if the wall is removed? Finding: the same handle becomes an ordinary spare part; the question lived in the relation, not the object alone.
Pull 4: what if the visitor has never seen a door? Finding: the handle/wall contrast no longer carries the same question; shared expectations are part of the exhibit.
Pull 5: could two objects ask the same question? Finding: a clock with thirteen equal marks and a staircase ending at a window both invite “which expectation did I supply?” despite different surfaces.
Pull 6: could one object ask opposed questions? Finding: the staircase can invite escape or confinement depending on which side is accessible; the exhibit needs a viewpoint.
Pull 7: what would a catalogue preserve? Finding: object, relation, expected norm, and viewing position—not merely an interrogative sentence.
Pull 8: what if an answer restores the ordinary relation? Finding: “the wall was moved yesterday” closes one historical question but leaves a new design question: why keep the handle? The exhibit can survive one answer by changing what it asks.

Tangents: a weather report for discarded questions; a cloakroom that returns a different uncertainty; an audio guide that asks the visitor to invent its missing last sentence. They are retained for pure interest; no immediate payoff is assigned.

What-if experiments: reverse the museum rule so answers must enter disguised as objects → a label becomes contraband; remove visitors → unresolved questions remain descriptions with no enacted viewpoint; combine museum and rehearsal room → exhibits can be rearranged and ask different questions tomorrow. The last hybrid connects inquiry to staging: relational position determines which contrast is available. This is a constructed analogy, not a psychological law.

Dead end: ranking exhibits by strangeness gives no principled distinction between a fresh question and random nonsense. It dies there; obscurity alone supplies no relation to inspect.

Discovery: an exploratory prompt can specify a violated expectation and a viewpoint before asking for an answer. Updated representation: question = answer request OR attention arrangement. Further exploration: yes, test the second representation on an ordinary social scene.''','I added “attention arrangement” as a question representation, with relation, expectation, and viewpoint fields.','The subsequent social prompt distinguishes two plausible interpretations without imputing either to a real person.','KEEP','The content gain is a new prompt form, not general proof that museums improve thinking. Organization stores the prompt’s relation and viewpoint so the surprising part survives its wording.','Build a sound-only question exhibit; use an ordinary object with no novelty; test a prompt whose apparent question disappears when viewpoint changes.',N+' Five hooks, eight dependent pulls, three tangents, three counterfactual experiments, a dead end, and a distinct later prompt.','New constructed scene: a colleague repeatedly closes a shared office door. Answer-request prompt: “Why do they dislike collaboration?” Revised attention arrangement: “From the corridor the closed door removes access; from the desk it removes noise. Which access expectation and work constraint are known in this case?” The produced output now contains two information-dependent interpretations and requests the missing fact. The initial motive-loaded question would have selected dislike as its premise. This is a local representation improvement, not evidence about the colleague.')
add('funr',2,'Curiosity can be reciprocal without being identical','Explore a conversation in which two people remain interested in different parts of the same object.','Shared curiosity initially suggests both parties should pursue the same question; I am not sure that common focus is necessary for a lively reciprocal exchange.', '''Constructed subject: two visitors find a folded paper boat. One wants to know whether it floats; the other wants to know who folded it. Hooks: same object, different questions; an answer for one can create a question for the other; an object can be jointly attended to without shared purpose; neither visitor has to abandon their question.

Thread, eight pulls: (1) Float it → folds loosen and a word appears. (2) The author question now has a clue that the buoyancy experiment produced accidentally. (3) Keeping it dry would preserve the clue’s arrangement but leave buoyancy unknown. (4) Photographing folds before wetting creates a third operation that serves both questions, but the photograph cannot preserve tactile details. (5) The first visitor can value the experiment while the second values the trace; agreement on sequence does not mean agreement on purpose. (6) If the second refuses wetting, the conflict is over an irreversible transformation, not interest intensity. (7) Making a replica tests one structural possibility without answering whether this particular boat floats. (8) Reciprocal inquiry therefore needs to preserve which object and which question each action concerns.

Tangents: a duet in which each player thinks the other is the accompaniment; a shared map drawn from opposite shores; a dinner in which everyone asks a question about a different ingredient. Pure-interest status remains; no social intervention has occurred.

What-ifs: reverse who values preservation → the same dispute swaps roles; give unlimited replicas → structural tests become cheap but historical uniqueness remains; combine paper boat with a message addressed to neither visitor → both question ownership and access become new topics.

Connections: experiments and conversation both alter what is available to the next participant; a cooperative sequence can carry incompatible ends if it preserves enough of each object. These are case structures, not empirical claims about people generally.

Dead end: calling the shared activity “collaboration” resolves none of the irreversible-action conflict. The label adds no choice.

Later application: generated exchange is “I want to learn about the folds; you want to test the water. Let’s record this boat before deciding whether to wet it. A replica could test the folding pattern, but not this boat.” It names separate questions and a limit. No actual participant accepts it.''','The constructed exchange can support distinct interests without pretending they are one interest; the human reciprocal effect remains untested.','A more precise conversational draft is available, but its useful difference from an actual baseline exchange has not been observed.','UNRESOLVED','Content separates shared object, separate questions, and irreversible action. Organization retains the disagreement instead of summarizing it as shared curiosity.','Explore a non-destructive shared object; let one visitor lose interest; test a dialogue where preserving both questions is itself unwanted.',N+' Four hooks, eight dependent pulls, three tangents, three reversals/hybrids, and a concrete dialogue.')
add('funr',3,'The useful boredom of a familiar object','Explore whether attention can change without increasing novelty or producing a new belief.','A fresh object or surprising fact seems the most likely route to an interesting exploration; an ordinary repeated shape offers little obvious material.', '''Subject: the same ordinary cup, described without inventing its history. Hooks: a handle is both gap and material; the rim can be a border or an opening; the cup is empty even when occupied by air; its front changes with the holder; a stain records use without narrating it.

Eight pulls along the handle: (1) The hole gives a finger a place while material gives the hand leverage. (2) Removing material can make an object easier to hold, up to the point of breakage. (3) The interesting property is not “more” or “less” material but placement. (4) Placement refers to a user’s relation to the cup; upside down, the same opening serves differently. (5) A drawing can preserve outline while hiding thickness, so two “same handles” differ in use. (6) Naming both “handle” compresses the difference that a practical test would reveal. (7) Suspending the label lets the opening, contour, and contact surfaces become separate attention targets. (8) Restoring the label now carries a richer representation, even if no fact about the cup has changed.

Tangents: a catalogue of useful holes; instructions for drawing only the spaces between objects; a room photographed from the level of its doorknobs. No usefulness score is assigned during the tangents.

Reversal experiments: a solid ring with no hole is no longer grasped by insertion; a cup with no rim is an incomplete boundary rather than a vessel; a cup combined with a calendar can use wear marks as entries, though this invented artwork supplies no reliable dates. Unexpected connection: categorization can both enable efficient reference and hide task-relevant differences in a case. The connection is representational, not a measured psychological effect.

Dead end: “the cup contains the universe” produces no concrete distinction and is discarded. It only sounds expansive.

Later application: describe an ordinary meeting agenda without its label. The output becomes “a sequence of permissions to speak, topics withheld until later, and decisions with different owners.” This changes the working description. Whether that description improves an actual meeting design is still open.

Certificate: the exploration produces a changed representation with unchanged object facts. Useful transfer would require the representation to change an appropriate design choice; the agenda description has not yet done so. A changed wording alone does not earn KEEP.''','My working description now includes contact relations and omitted spaces; the later agenda description shows representational transfer but not demonstrated useful consequence.','An additional attention target is available; no practical or human benefit has been established.','UNRESOLVED','Content removes the unsupported cosmic tangent. Organization preserves the “remove label, retain concrete relations” candidate as untested rather than a general rule.','Use the agenda description in a real allocation decision; explore an object whose label is essential; test whether added relational detail distracts from a simple task.',N+' Five hooks, eight dependent pulls, three tangents, three reversals, a rejected dead end, and a later representation.')
